ports, the contract task is not needed\n\nBut the constraint is explicit about \"new package\". Let me just follow it literally and propose a contract task for nl.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the task limit. I have 4 tasks max. If I spend 1 on a tac completion and 1 on an nl contract, that leaves only 2 for nl implementation—which feels wasteful when the contract for a cmd/ package would be trivial anyway. I think the pragmatic move is to combine the contract into the first implementation task for nl since there's nothing meaningful to contract without exported types.\n\nNow I need to check whether the project already has these directories. The code_status shows \"not started\" for all of 05.0, but the recent commits suggest tac work has already begun, so I should verify what actually exists in the source. I need to include a contract task for nl before any implementation tasks, since the package doesn't exist yet. The contract task for a cmd/ package would define the main function stub and flag parsing for all nl options, so I'll restructure to: finish tac, create the nl contract with flag parsing stubs, then tackle the implementation groups in sequence. I need to make sure the stitch agent can read the SRD files directly to understand what each requirement entails, rather than me trying to paraphrase them. Let me verify the constraint on sub-requirements per task—I've got three groups for nl with 4 sub-requirements each and one group for tac with 3, which all fit within the 4 sub-requirement limit. Now I'll draft the actual task output.\n\nFor the contract task, I need to anchor it to specific SRD requirement IDs even though it's more about package structure. I think I can tie it to the nl requirements since it's foundational work that supports those.\n\nLooking at the dependencies, tasks 0 and 1 can run in parallel since they're independent, while tasks 2 and 3 need to be sequential because task 2 sets up the initial implementation that task 3 builds on.\n\nDe