raints—each task stays within the 4-point limit, and I'm assuming default weights of 1 for all sub-requirements since the requirements file isn't available. This looks solid.\n\nI should be specific about what the stitch agent needs to do for each task: for tac, it extends the existing code and adds test entries; for the nl contract, it reads the SRD and creates the main file with stubs; then the implementation tasks build on that foundation. I also need to make sure the required_reading includes the SRD and any other files the agent should reference.\n\nLooking at the code_status, all four use cases are spec-complete but not started, so I'm working with a clean slate here.\n\nThe key insight is that requirement_states only tracks items with \"ready\" status — echo's SRD requirements aren't listed there, which means they've either been completed already or aren't marked as ready. Since the constraint specifies I should only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status, I'm correctly skipping echo and the other items not appearing in requirement_states like logname and nproc.\n\nRelease 05.0 is indeed the earliest release with ready requirements — all earlier releases have incomplete items but none marked as ready. Within 05.0, I can fit tac's three ready requirements and start nl's implementation within the four-task limit, leaving fold for the next batch.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to split implementation and tests into separate tasks. The constraint says to always split them, but the project's recent pattern combines them for cmd/ packages where tests are just DiffTest declarations. Given that tac R4.1-R4.3 would only be 50-130 lines total, splitting would create two tiny tasks. I think the practical approach is to combine impl and tests for these small cmd/ packages — the constraint about splitting applies more to complex unit tests in pkg/ packages. I'm going with combined impl+tests following the established project pattern.\n\nFor the nl contract task, I need to create the skeleton files with the option struct, flag parsing stubs, and test file setup so the implementation tasks have a clear starting point.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the broader project context—go-unix-utils is systematically reimplementing GNU utilities with differential testing across 114 specifications. Release 05.0 is currently in progress with echo complete, tac partially done, and nl and fold both ready with all 16 and 15 requirements respectively waiting for implementation.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit, my strategy is to finish tac's remaining 3 requirements first since it's closest to completion, then move to nl's contract and initial implementation phases covering the first two requirement groups.\n\nFor the nl contract task, I realize I need to anchor it to the SRD requirements it supports — the task requirements themselves (like struct definitions and function stubs) map back to the SRD requirements that nl will eventually implement, so I can reference those as the anchoring point.\n\nLooking at how previous tasks were structured in this project, I should keep the contract and implementation together in one task rather than splitting them, and make sure the description explicitly calls out that test cases are included.\n\nFor the required_reading section, I need to reference the spec files and existing code patterns from the repo root, particularly the testutils difftest module that defines the testing pattern I'll be following. the primary type is DiffTest, the file is difftest.go\"). So: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n- pkg/sys/ — for InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n\nI should also check if there's a test suite file. The docs structure suggests test suites live at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-[release-id].yaml, so for release 05.0 that would be docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-05.0.yaml. I'm not certain it exists, but I'll include it as optional context. Really though, the stitch agent needs to read the SRD first to understand the requirements—that's the core reference material.\n\nNow looking at the actual priorities: tac is nearly finished with just 3 requirements left, so that's the immediate focus. After that, nl and fold both have their requirements ready to go, so they're next in line. The project is structured around this four-stage pipeline, and we're currently working through release 05.0 which covers text stream transforms.\n\nLet me finalize the task structure with proper weight budgeting and make sure each task is self-contained and follows the constitution.\n\nI'm being careful about design decisions here — I should only include choices that come from the architecture documentation or are general project patterns, not arbitrary implementation details. Since I can't access the SRDs directly, I'll note that the stitch agent should reference them for specific design choices.\n\nLooking at the srd022-nl requirements, I see R1 through R4 are all marked as ready, so I need to verify which ones fit within the weight constraints for the proposed tasks.\n\nFor srd021-tac, only three requirements remain to be completed.\n\nNow I'm applying the ordering constraint P8 to ensure tasks are proposed in the right sequence — documentation first, then types and interfaces before implementations, shared packages before consumers, and implementations before tests.\n\nSince tac is nearly done with just three requirements left, it should come first to clear out that work. Then I'll tackle the nl contract to establish the new package structure, followed by nl's implementation groups in order starting with R1. I'm realizing that rule 2 about types before implementations probably applies within a single package, not across different packages. So the right approach is to order packages alphabetically by file path, then within each package handle contracts before implementations — which means cmd/nl comes first with its contract and implementations, followed by cmd/tac. I need to order tasks by contracts first, then implementations,